  "summary_text": "Kyle,\n\nPort Washington delivered yesterday\u2014up 16% total, and more importantly, core grooming revenue jumped 28% on five active groomers running $590 per groomer-day, well above your 30-day baseline of $490. That's execution. The team (Danielle, Jackie, Joyce, Olivia, Paige) moved dogs efficiently. However, two operational signals merit attention: add-on attach fell from 68% to 57%, and retail dropped 18%. This isn't a capacity story; it's a front-of-house discipline gap. Your lapse pool at Port Washington is substantial\u2014516 dormant or drifting customers representing $318k in annual value. That's your reactivation runway.\n\nHicksville is the outlier and the story. October finished up 22% versus September and 8.7% ahead of DOW-adjusted expectation\u2014real performance, not calendar luck. You're running 2.9 groomers per day (up from 2.6 last month) at $415 per groomer, flat to baseline but with fewer active staff. That's efficiency. The concern: retail cratered 69% yesterday, and you've got negative inventory on X-Small Treat Tables (\u2212191 units) and Cannoli (\u2212107 units). Stock out means lost upsell and customer friction. Fix that this week.\n\nGlen Cove is a watch item. Two groomers producing $150 per groomer-day\u201435% below baseline\u2014suggests either low-skilled assignment or thin scheduling yesterday. Retail spiked 90%, but that's noise against soft grooming volume. You have zero grooming coverage scheduled for today and tomorrow, which feels unintentional.\n\n**Port Washington: 4 groomers + 8h bathers today, 1 groomer + 15h bathers tomorrow, 36 groomer-days next 7 days; Hicksville: 4 groomers + 0h bathers today, 0 groomers tomorrow, 22 groomer-days next 7 days; Glen Cove: 0 groomer coverage today and tomorrow, 0 groomer-days next 7 days.**"
